<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalpa Sūtra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kalpa Sutra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kalpa Sūtra is a Jain scripture containing the biographies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankaras</a>, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parshvanatha">Parshvanatha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>. It is ascribed to Acharya Bhadrabahu, which would place it in the 4th century BCE, and it states that it was written down 980 or 993 years after Mahavira's nirvana. From the mid-15th century it was frequently illustrated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature paintings</a>, and the oldest surviving copies are on paper from western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 14th century. It is read during the eight-day festival of Paryushan, and Jain tradition regards the sound of its words as transforming the listener's soul.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalpa-sutra.jpg" alt="Kalpa Sūtra" width="330" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalpasutra_Mahavira_Nirvana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in Gujarat, in 453 or 466 CE, where it was fixed in the form known today: the Agamas, about forty-five texts in Ardhamagadhi Prakrit, among them the Acaranga Sutra, which describes Mahavira’s ascetic life, and the <b>Kalpa Sutra</b>, recited during Paryushana, which tells the lives of the Tirthankaras.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
